<div class="election-positions">
    <h2 class="fw-bold mb-4">Municipal Positions</h2>
    
    <section class="position-role mb-4">
        <h3>Mayor</h3>
        <p>
            The mayor is the head and chief executive officer (CEO) of the municipality. While they possess a single vote just like other council members, the mayor has additional leadership duties. They are responsible for chairing council meetings, providing general direction to municipal staff, and establishing standing committees. The mayor also acts as the primary public representative for the municipality at ceremonies and events. However, a mayor cannot act unilaterally; all policies and decisions require a majority vote from the council.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="position-role mb-4">
        <h3>Councillor</h3>
        <p>
            Councillors balance the needs of their constituents with the long-term well-being of the municipality. Along with the mayor, they form the municipal council, which acts collectively to pass bylaws and resolutions. Councillors vote on key community issues, including zoning, property taxes, budgets, and public services like policing, fire, and garbage collection. They also sit on various committees to develop and evaluate municipal policies and programs.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="position-role">
        <h3>School Trustee</h3>
        <p>
            Elected in accordance with the Local Government Act and School Act, trustees form the local Board of Education. Their primary mandate is to ensure the delivery and quality of educational services for students in their district. The board acts as a collective corporation; individual trustees do not have independent authority. They work together to set the district's vision, establish educational policies, oversee the school district's budget, and hire the superintendent.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>